ALTERNATING GROUP GRAPH AG_n vs the bipartite transposition graph of S_n
========================================================================
  AG_3: |A_n|=3 vertices, (2n-4)=2-regular, bipartite=False, odd-girth=3, chi=3
  AG_4: |A_n|=12 vertices, (2n-4)=4-regular, bipartite=False, odd-girth=3, chi=3
  AG_5: |A_n|=60 vertices, (2n-4)=6-regular, bipartite=False, odd-girth=3, chi=3
  AG_6: |A_n|=360 vertices, (2n-4)=8-regular, bipartite=False, odd-girth=(skip), chi=(skip)

  contrast: Cay(S_n, transpositions) is bipartite (sign map) => chi=2.
  S_4 transposition graph: |S_4|=24, bipartite=True (chi=2, the sign 2-coloring)

  => AG_n is the PERMANENTLY-ODD (non-bipartite) rung: A_n = ker(sign), no parity 2-coloring,
     so chi(AG_n)>=3 for all n. The alternating group is where parity runs out (S587 odd sector).
